memory: mtk-smi: mt6797's SMI_LARB_MMU_EN is at 0xfc0, not 0xf00

memory/0001 added the mt6797 larb by pointing its compatible at
mtk_smi_larb_mt8173, "modelled on mt6795". The register offset that
choice carries is wrong for this SoC, and it is the one thing in that
structure that matters: the mt8167 and mt8173 larb_gen entries differ in
nothing except which address config_port writes the per-port MMU enables
to.

The vendor M4U register map for this exact SoC,
drivers/misc/mediatek/m4u/mt6797/m4u_reg.h, is unambiguous:

    #define SMI_LARB_MMU_EN  (0xfc0)
    #define SMI_LARB_SEC_EN  (0xfc4)

0xfc0 is what mainline names MT8167_SMI_LARB_MMU_EN. MT8173's is 0xf00.

The failure this produces is worth describing, because the log actively
misleads. With the enables written to the wrong register every port stays
in IOMMU bypass, so once the display components are attached to a domain
the OVL receives IOVAs and treats them as physical addresses. The screen
garbles -- and NOT ONE IOMMU fault is logged, because the transactions
never reach the IOMMU. A clean dmesg is exactly what you get, and it is
the opposite of reassuring.

Found by flashing the display's iommus properties (dts/0042) and having
the owner report a garbled panel while the kernel reported a clean bind
and zero faults.
--- a/drivers/memory/mtk-smi.c
+++ b/drivers/memory/mtk-smi.c
@@ -403,6 +403,29 @@
 	.config_port = mtk_smi_larb_config_port_mt8167,
 };
 
+static const struct mtk_smi_larb_gen mtk_smi_larb_mt6797 = {
+	/*
+	 * MT6797's SMI_LARB_MMU_EN lives at 0xfc0, NOT the 0xf00 that MT8173
+	 * and MT6795 use. From the vendor M4U register map for this exact SoC,
+	 * drivers/misc/mediatek/m4u/mt6797/m4u_reg.h:
+	 *
+	 *     #define SMI_LARB_MMU_EN  (0xfc0)
+	 *     #define SMI_LARB_SEC_EN  (0xfc4)
+	 *
+	 * 0xfc0 is the offset mainline happens to name MT8167_SMI_LARB_MMU_EN,
+	 * so that config_port is the correct one to reuse; the larb_gen entries
+	 * for mt8167 and mt8173 differ in nothing else.
+	 *
+	 * Getting this wrong is silent and vicious, and it cost a flash to find.
+	 * The per-port MMU enables land in some other register, every port stays
+	 * in bypass, and the display then reads the IOVAs the IOMMU handed it as
+	 * if they were physical addresses. The screen garbles with ZERO IOMMU
+	 * faults logged -- because the transactions never reach the IOMMU at all,
+	 * which makes the log look reassuring at exactly the wrong moment.
+	 */
+	.config_port = mtk_smi_larb_config_port_mt8167,
+};
+
 static const struct mtk_smi_larb_gen mtk_smi_larb_mt8173 = {
 	/* mt8173 do not need the port in larb */
 	.config_port = mtk_smi_larb_config_port_mt8173,
@@ -442,7 +465,7 @@
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt2712-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt2712},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6779-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt6779},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6795-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt8173},
-	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt8173},
+	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt6797},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt8167-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt8167},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt8173-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt8173},
 	{.compatible = "mediatek,mt8183-smi-larb", .data = &mtk_smi_larb_mt8183},
